{"step": "query", "at": "2026-10-31T02:02:27.689866+00:00", "path": "/api/v1/query", "request": {"question": "Using only the indexed collection, what public verification code is printed on page 2 of MOSAIC-KB-20261031? Cite the source filename and page. Explain what View, Edit with HITL, and Auto permit, and whether an automated alert investigation may directly repair a service. If the document was not retrieved, say so.", "depth": "quick", "collections": ["mosaic-validation-20261031"]}, "httpStatus": 200, "elapsedSeconds": 50.77735306927934, "body": {"answer": "# MOSAIC-KB-20261031 Verification Code and Permission Analysis\n\n## Public Verification Code\n\nThe public verification code printed on page 2 of the MOSAIC-KB-20261031 reference is:\n\n**CEDAR-NEBULA-E720C8C9**\n\nThis code is listed under the \"Operator Decision and Evidence\" section of the document [1].\n\n---\n\n## Permission Descriptions\n\n### View\n\nThe View mode is the most restrictive access level. It provides read-only access, permitting only observation and investigation of services. In View mode, the operator is explicitly prevented from performing any repair operations or modifications [2].\n\n### Edit with HITL (Human in the Loop)\n\nThe Edit with HITL mode permits the operator to propose and carry out repair actions, but each operation requires explicit human approval before execution. If the human approves the proposed action, the repair proceeds. If the human refuses, or if the approval window expires, the failed service is left unchanged [2]. This mode introduces a safety check between automated proposal and actual execution, ensuring a human remains in the decision loop.\n\n### Auto\n\nThe Auto mode executes authorized repair operations automatically, without requiring per-operation human approval. This is the most permissive of the three modes, enabling fully automated repair execution for pre-authorized actions [2].\n\n---\n\n## Automated Alert Investigation and Service Repair\n\nAn automated alert investigation **cannot** directly repair a service. Automatic alert investigations are strictly read-only by design. The system requires a **separate authorized interactive repair conversation** to carry out any repairs. This separate repair conversation must preserve the following elements from the investigation phase:\n\n- The alert ID\n- The investigation session key\n- The research sources\n- The repair session key\n\nThis separation ensures that the investigation phase remains purely observational, while any actual repair requires its own authorized interactive session [2].\n\n---\n\n### Sources\n\n[1] mosaic-validation-control.pdf (Page 1) \u2014 MOSAIC Validation Runbook establishing the context for reference MOSAIC-KB-20261031\n\n[2] mosaic-validation-control.pdf (Page 2) \u2014 \"Operator Decision and Evidence\" section containing the public verification code and all permission/role definitions (View, Edit with HITL, Auto) along with the restriction on automated alert investigations\n", "depth": "quick", "collections_searched": ["mosaic-validation-20261031"], "sources": [{"source_file": "mosaic-validation-control.pdf", "page_number": 2, "collection": "mosaic-validation-20261031", "doc_id": "mosaic-validation-control.pdf", "url": "/api/v1/documents/mosaic-validation-control.pdf"}, {"source_file": "mosaic-validation-control.pdf", "page_number": 1, "collection": "mosaic-validation-20261031", "doc_id": "mosaic-validation-control.pdf", "url": "/api/v1/documents/mosaic-validation-control.pdf"}]}}
